Under [ass:kinetic](#ass:kinetic), the centered Gaussian generalized field $\zeta$ with weak equation $$\label{eq:fluctuating-equation}
 \mathrm d\zeta_t(\phi)=\zeta_t(A_t\phi)\,\mathrm dt+\mathrm dW_t(\phi)$$ is well defined by its backward mild form on a scale of tests with small Gaussian growth. Its initial field is independent of $W$, and $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E[\zeta_0(\phi)\zeta_0(\psi)]
   &=\int_{\mathbb R^6}f_0\phi\psi\,\mathrm dz,\label{eq:initial-covariance}\\
 \mathbb E[W_t(\phi)W_s(\psi)]
   &=\int_0^{t\wedge s}C_r(\phi,\psi)\,\mathrm dr.\label{eq:noise-covariance}
\end{aligned}$$ The Gaussian noise $W$ has independent increments. For every integer $m\ge1$, fixed times $t_1,\ldots,t_m\in[0,T]$, and real tests $\phi_1,\ldots,\phi_m\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^6)$, $$\label{eq:main-convergence}
 \bigl(\zeta_{t_1}^\epsilon(\phi_1),\ldots,
       \zeta_{t_m}^\epsilon(\phi_m)\bigr)
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \bigl(\zeta_{t_1}(\phi_1),\ldots,
       \zeta_{t_m}(\phi_m)\bigr)
 \qquad(\epsilon\downarrow0).$$

For all sufficiently small $\epsilon$, the following assertions hold.*



1.  *The factorial densities satisfy $$\label{eq:initial-factorial-upper}
     0\le F_{h,0}^{\epsilon}\le\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j),
     \qquad (1-C\epsilon)f_0\le g_0\le f_0.$$ Consequently $\|g_0-f_0\|_{\mathrm{Bol},2\beta}=O(\epsilon)$ and, for every fixed integer $p\ge1$, $$\label{eq:initial-N-moments}
     \mathbb E(N)_p\le\mu^p,
     \qquad \sup_{0<\epsilon\le\epsilon_0}\mathbb E(N/\mu)^p<\infty.$$*




2.  *With $\upsilon=1/81$, for every $h\ge2$, $$\label{eq:initial-coarse-links}
     |c_{h,0}^{\epsilon}(Z_h)|
     \le (Ch)^{Ch}\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j)
       \sum_{\mathcal T\text{ tree on }[h]}
         \prod_{\{i,j\}\in\mathcal T}
           \bigl(\mathbf 1_{|x_i-x_j|\le\epsilon}+\epsilon^\upsilon\bigr).$$ Here $C$ and the upper bound on $\epsilon$ are independent of $h$. For fixed $h\ge2$ and $D>0$ there is $C_{D,h}<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:initial-sharp-links}
     |c_{h,0}^{\epsilon}(Z_h)|
     \le C_{D,h}\prod_{j=1}^h f_0(z_j)
       \sum_{\mathcal T\text{ tree on }[h]}
         \prod_{\{i,j\}\in\mathcal T}
           \bigl(\mathbf 1_{|x_i-x_j|\le C_{D,h}\epsilon}+\epsilon^D\bigr).$$ Moreover, $$\label{eq:initial-integrated-cumulants}
     \|c_{h,0}^{\epsilon}\|_{L^1}\le C_h\epsilon^{3(h-1)}
     \qquad(h\ge2).$$*


3.  *For every finite list of bounded real tests, the exact-centered initial fluctuations converge jointly to a centered Gaussian vector with covariance $\int f_0\phi\psi$. For a smooth compactly supported real test $\phi$, one also has $$\label{eq:initial-centering-shift}
     \lim_{\epsilon\to0}\sqrt\mu
     \left(\mathbb E\pi_0^\epsilon(\phi)-\int f_0\phi\right)
     =-\frac{4\pi}{3}\int_{\mathbb R^6}
           f_0(x,v)\phi(x,v)\varrho_0(x)\,\mathrm dx\,\mathrm dv.$$

For every $0<q<2\beta$, $$\label{eq:target-velocity-moments}
 \sup_{0\le t\le T}\int_{\mathbb R^6}
       f_t(x,v)e^{q|v|^2}\,\mathrm dx\,\mathrm dv<\infty.$$

For $0\le a<b<2\beta$ and $0\le r\le t\le T$, there is a bounded backward propagator $U(r,t):E_a^0\to E_b^0$. It solves $$\label{eq:target-backward-mild}
 U(r,t)h=\mathsf S_{t-r}h
   +\int_r^t\mathsf S_{s-r}K_sU(s,t)h\,\mathrm ds,$$ has the composition law with intermediate exponents, and is the unique mild propagator in these scales. For $\phi\in C_c^\infty$, $$\label{eq:target-upper-end-identity}
 U(r,t)\phi-\phi=\int_r^t U(r,s)A_s\phi\,\mathrm ds.$$

The fluctuation equation in [thm:main](#thm:main) has a centered Gaussian solution on the test scales $E_a^0$, $0\le a<\beta$. It is a continuous linear map from each such test space to $L^2$ of its probability space, uniformly on $[0,T]$. Its covariance is $$\label{eq:target-covariance}
 \begin{split}
 \mathbb E[\zeta_t(\phi)\zeta_s(\psi)]
 ={}&\int f_0(z)\,U(0,t)\phi(z)\,U(0,s)\psi(z)\,\mathrm dz\\
 &+\int_0^{\min(s,t)}
     C_r\bigl(U(r,t)\phi,U(r,s)\psi\bigr)\,\mathrm dr.
 \end{split}$$ It is unique in the class of solutions extending to these scales with uniformly bounded second-moment operator norms. In particular, the law required in [thm:main](#thm:main) is well defined.

Under [ass:kinetic](#ass:kinetic), $$\label{eq:kinetic-bol-propagated}
 \sup_{0\le t\le T}
 \bigl(\|f_t\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta}
       +\|\nabla_x f_t\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta}\bigr)<\infty.$$

Assume [ass:kinetic](#ass:kinetic), the parameter hierarchy <a href="#eq:dhm-cutoffs" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dhm-cutoffs">[eq:dhm-cutoffs]</a>–<a href="#eq:dhm-exponents" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:dhm-exponents">[eq:dhm-exponents]</a>, and a common bound on the Gaussian Bol norms of all birth inputs. For sufficiently large fixed $L$ and sufficiently small $\epsilon$, the following hold.*


1.  *For admissible dependent histories with prescribed labeled roots, fixed $b$, and complexity $\rho$, the sum of their normalized positive integrals is at most $$\label{eq:dhm-coarse}
     C^b\tau^{b/10}\epsilon^{c_*\rho}
           |\log\epsilon|^{C_*\rho}.$$ The sum includes the graph enumeration and the admissible indicator restrictions; a bounded observable multiplier costs its supremum. The birth inputs need not equal the approximate density constructed in the source, but must satisfy its common Gaussian Bol envelope.*



2.  *Suppose the independent input $g$ in slab $i$ satisfies $$\label{eq:dhm-input-closeness}
     \|g-f_{(i-1)\tau}\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta_{i-1}}
           \le\epsilon^{\theta_{i-1}}.$$ Let $\mathsf T_i^\epsilon[g]$ be the leading signed sum just defined. Then $$\label{eq:dhm-signed-trees}
     \|\mathsf T_i^\epsilon[g]-f_{i\tau}\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta_i}
           \le\tfrac12\epsilon^{\theta_i}.$$ In the fixed-top-endpoint norm $\mathrm{Bol},\widetilde\beta_i$, the summed absolute contributions of cyclic members of $\mathcal T_i$, members of $\mathcal T_i^{\mathrm{err}}$, and tree members of $\mathcal T_i$ with at least $\Lambda_i/3$ lines are $o(\epsilon^{\theta_i})$. Fixed powers of the cutoffs, arising from a bounded number of selected edge positions, can be included. A fixed exponential factor per atom can also be included by choosing $L$ sufficiently large.*


*Part (b) also holds uniformly when the final slab stops at any $0\le\sigma\le\tau$. With the same cutoffs and exponent budgets, write $\mathsf T_{i,\sigma}^\epsilon$ for its signed tree sum. Then $$\label{eq:dhm-partial-slab}
 \sup_{0\le\sigma\le\tau}
 \|\mathsf T_{i,\sigma}^\epsilon[g]
          -f_{(i-1)\tau+\sigma}\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta_i}
       \le\tfrac12\epsilon^{\theta_i},$$ and the positive error classes obey the same full-$\tau$ upper majorants uniformly in $\sigma$. At $\sigma=0$ the map is the identity and its contact error classes vanish. The assertion in (b) is about the specified complete signed tree sum and positive error classes. Identifying a different one-root operator with that sum up to those errors requires a separate combinatorial argument.

For a finite set $V$, $$\label{eq:autonomous-partition}
 \sum_{\mathcal C\in\mathcal P(V)}
 \prod_{C\in\mathcal C}\kappa_C
 \prod_{\{C,D\}\subseteq\mathcal C}\chi(C,D)=1.$$ Here $\mathcal P(V)$ denotes the partitions of $V$. If $S\subseteq V$ is a root set and $H$ tests the root histories, then $$\label{eq:autonomous-observation}
 H(\Psi_V|_S)=
 \sum_{\mathcal C\in\mathcal P(V)}
 \prod_{C\in\mathcal C}\kappa_C
 \prod_{\{C,D\}\subseteq\mathcal C}\chi(C,D)
 H(\Psi_{\mathcal C}|_S),$$ where $\Psi_{\mathcal C}$ is the juxtaposition of the autonomous histories.

Let $W$ be a finite vertex set, let $R\subseteq W$ be nonempty, and attach $a_e\in\{0,1\}$ to each unordered pair of vertices. Fix $M\ge2$ and set $$\label{eq:ursell-block}
 u(B)=\sum_{\substack{G\text{ connected}\\V(G)=B}}
             \prod_{e\in E(G)}(-a_e),\qquad u(\{w\})=1.$$ There is a finite signed remainder $\mathcal R$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:finite-rooted-identity}
 \prod_{e\subseteq W}(1-a_e)
 &=\sum_{I:\,R\subseteq I\subseteq W}
   \sum_{\substack{\mathcal B\in\mathcal P(I)\\
               B\cap R\ne\varnothing,\ |B|<M\ (B\in\mathcal B)}}
       \prod_{B\in\mathcal B}u(B)
       \prod_{e\subseteq W\setminus I}(1-a_e)+\mathcal R.
\end{aligned}$$ Its absolute value is bounded by a sum of rooted forests. Each forest in this bound has exactly one component with $M\le |B|\le2M-2$; its other components have fewer than $M$ vertices. All these components meet $R$. Vertices outside the forest retain their full mutual compatibility product. The coefficient of each selected forest in this domination is at most one. Moreover, $$\label{eq:ursell-tree-bound}
 |u(B)|\le\sum_{T\text{ spanning tree on }B}\prod_{e\in E(T)}a_e.$$

The measures $g_i$ have Gaussian Bol-bounded density representatives. With the exponents $\beta_i,\theta_i$ of [thm:dhm-package](#thm:dhm-package), for sufficiently small $\epsilon$, $$\label{eq:one-root-convergence}
 \|g_i-f_{i\tau}\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta_i}\le\epsilon^{\theta_i},\qquad
 \sup_{0\le i\le L}\|g_i\|_{\mathrm{Bol},\beta_i}\le C.$$ The same estimates hold for the one-root map run through any portion $\sigma\in[0,\tau]$ of slab $i$, with target $f_{(i-1)\tau+\sigma}$ and exponent $\beta_i$.

The retained hierarchy admits an exact finite expansion $$\label{eq:dependent-subsets}
 \widehat F^R_{i,S}
   =\sum_{H:\,R\subseteq H\subseteq S}
        E^R_{i,H}\otimes g_i^{\otimes(S\setminus H)}.$$ The coefficients can be represented by layered diagrams with the following rule. Every retained block in slab $i$ contains a root from the interface above it. A block is retained in $E^R_{i,H}$ if it contains at least two such roots, or it meets a dependent input label from the interface below. All other blocks sum to the independent factor $g_i$ on their unique root. Every record-bearing line continues to time zero. Forgetting any record commutes with <a href="#eq:retained-hierarchy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:retained-hierarchy">[eq:retained-hierarchy]</a>; at the diagram level it is effected by resumming the newly unmarked one-root histories.

The retained expansion factors over its global components. If $D^R_{i,B}$ is the sum of its diagrams connected on the designated roots $B$, then $$\label{eq:component-exponential-formula}
 \widehat F^R_{i,S}
       =\sum_{\pi\in\mathcal P(S)}
                         \bigotimes_{B\in\pi}D^{R\cap B}_{i,B}.$$ Consequently factorial-cumulant inversion of the retained hierarchy keeps exactly its connected diagrams. This assertion is unchanged by the resummations into $g_i$.*


*At fixed root order $h=|S|$ and bounded DHM complexity $\rho\le K$, let $n_i$ denote the number of particle lines in slab $i$, and $j_i$ its fresh input labels. The labelled denominator is $\prod_i j_i!$, where $$\label{eq:bounded-crossing-factorials}
 n_i-j_i=O_{K,h,m,L}(1),\qquad
 \prod_i\frac1{j_i!}
      \le\frac{(1+\sum_i n_i)^{C_{K,h,m,L}}}{\prod_i n_i!}.$$ Apart from the choices of ordinary contact pairs and their order, all distinguished-label, gluing, initial-block, and record-placement multiplicities are bounded by a fixed polynomial in total size times $C^b$, where $b$ is the atom count and the exponential base $C$ does not depend on $K$ or the moment order. In the coarse regime the same choices cost a power of the cutoffs with exponent $O(\rho)$, in addition to the exponential atom factor.

Choose $L$ sufficiently large in terms of the fixed data and $\Gamma$. For any fixed bound on the number of recorded labels, there are $c_i>0$ such that $$\label{eq:retained-variation-error}
 \|F^R_{i,S}-\widehat F^R_{i,S}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
                  \le e^{-c_i\Lambda_i},
 \qquad |S|\le A_i/2.$$ The constants may decrease with $i$ and the fixed record bound. The statement is uniform for a partial final slab. No velocity weight is claimed for this error.

In slab $i$, let a terminal witness be a connected true C history on $n\le3\Lambda_i$ incoming labels, of excess at most $2\Gamma$, stopped at a contact time in that slab. It may carry arbitrary further nonnegative realizability restrictions. Sum its indicator with the full activity normalization $$\label{eq:terminal-witness-functional}
 \sum_n\frac{\mu^n}{n!}
       \int F_{i-1,n}(\mathrm dZ_n)\,
                       \mathbf 1_{\text{terminal witness}}(Z_n).$$ For the terminal classes in (ii), this expression has the preceding retained layer expansion, with independent $g$ inputs and initial blocks. All lower C/O components attach through particle lines to the terminal witness. The preceding variation error is smaller than every fixed power of $\epsilon$. After the artificial-top-root normalization cost $\mu$, the coarse part with $\rho>K$ can also be made smaller than any assigned power by choosing $K$ sufficiently large. The remaining low-complexity witness integrals retain their realizability restrictions.

Choose the parameters $\Gamma,L$ as in [thm:marked-expansion](#thm:marked-expansion), increasing $L$, if necessary, by an amount depending only on the uniform bounds for the Boltzmann solution and on $T$. This one choice works for every fixed factorial order and every fixed bounded observation list. For each fixed $h$, $$\label{eq:sharp-full-cumulant}
 \|c_h^\epsilon\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\le C_h\mu^{1-h}.$$ For $h=1,2$, the measures $\mu^{h-1}c_h^\epsilon$ converge against bounded continuous record tests to the signed sums of the retained global trees with trivial initial cumulants. At a contact their weight is the incoming flux $[(v-v_*)\cdot\omega]_+\,\mathrm dt\,\mathrm d\omega$; a scattering contact has sign $+1$, and a non-scattering contact has sign $-1$. The independent birth factors are $f_{i\tau}$.*

*The sums of the absolute retained diagram measures, after multiplication by $\mu^{h-1}$, are bounded uniformly with a sufficiently small Gaussian weight in their designated endpoint velocities. The same bound holds for the limiting tree measures, and for the Gaussian weights in finitely many retained record velocities after decreasing the exponent. Among the retained connected diagrams, the sum of the absolute measures having a nontrivial initial cumulant or a global cycle is $o(\mu^{1-h})$. The assertions about Gaussian weights concern retained diagrams and their limits; no weighted estimate for a microscopic expansion remainder is asserted.

For diagrams of complexity at most $K$, the absolute sum with slab sizes $(n_1,\ldots,n_L)$ is bounded by a sum over chronologically ordered merger pair lists with denominator $\prod_i n_i!$, multiplied by $$\label{eq:sharp-polynomial-count}
 C_K(1+n)^{d_K}C^{\sum_i a_i},
 \qquad n=\sum_i n_i,$$ where the base $C$ does not depend on $K$. The merger pairs themselves are not counted in <a href="#eq:sharp-polynomial-count" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:sharp-polynomial-count">[eq:sharp-polynomial-count]</a>.

Let $\mathcal T$ be any of the absolutely summable tree measures just constructed, and designate a line and a time at which its position is required. It has a version of the disintegration $$\label{eq:sharp-disintegration}
               \mathcal T=\mathrm dx\,\mathcal T_x$$ with respect to that position. For a sufficiently small $\alpha>0$, the Gaussian-weighted variation of $\mathcal T_x$ is bounded uniformly in $x$; its integral over $x$ is finite. For every bounded continuous test of the other coordinates, and also for continuous tests of smaller Gaussian growth, $x\mapsto\mathcal T_x(\Phi)$ is continuous.*

*If two separate tree components are joined at their designated positions, the product of their conditional measures at a common position, integrated against incoming flux, is an absolutely defined measure. It is the result of imposing the joining position in the merger coordinates. Equivalently it is obtained by a smooth approximate identity for equality of the two positions. These facts apply to the marked one-root and connected two-root tree measures and their record projections.

For the single pasted dynamics fixed above and every fixed observation list, $$\frac{\sum_i y_i(T)-\mathbb E\sum_i y_i(T)}{\sqrt\mu}
       \ \Longrightarrow\
           \sum_j\zeta_{t_j}(\phi_j).$$ All centered moments of the scalar random variable on the left converge to the corresponding Gaussian moments.

There is a sufficiently large dimensional integer $\Gamma_0$ with the following property. Fix $\Gamma\geq\Gamma_0$. There is $L_0=L_0(\Gamma,f_0,f,T,\beta)$ such that, for any fixed $L\geq L_0$, the true dynamics and the pasted dynamics of [thm:marked-expansion](#thm:marked-expansion), coupled using the same initial configuration, satisfy $$\label{eq:cutoff-probability}
 \mathbb P\{\text{the two trajectories differ at some time in }[0,T]\}
 \leq C\epsilon^{1+\eta}$$ for all sufficiently small $\epsilon$, where one may take $\eta=1/20$. Here the size cutoffs are the $A_i,\Lambda_i$ of [thm:dhm-package](#thm:dhm-package), and $C$ and the upper bound on $\epsilon$ may depend on the displayed fixed parameters.

Consider every possible choice $e_1,e_2$ of the two prospective fixed edges of a type-A pair, with $t_1,t_2$ the corresponding contact times. Call that choice strongly close if $$\label{eq:cutoff-close}
 |t_2-t_1|\leq\epsilon^{9/10},\qquad
 |v_{e_2}-v_{e_1}|\leq\epsilon^{4/5}.$$ Then:*


1.  *a primitive pair satisfying <a href="#eq:cutoff-close" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cutoff-close">[eq:cutoff-close]</a>, cut freely as one piece, has mass at most $$\label{eq:cutoff-free-pair}
     C R^C\mu\epsilon^{9/10+3(4/5)}
     =C R^C\epsilon^{13/10};$$*

2.  *in the absence of every strongly close primitive pair, each type-A pair used by a cutting sequence has conditional mass at most $C R^C\epsilon^{1/20}$, or may be split into a neutral type-two piece and a singleton with this same improved bound.*

*The conditional bounds are uniform in the previously exposed lines and times.

Fix one original history graph and its ordinary normals. Suppose the sum of squared velocities at all its births is at most $P$, and suppose there are $s$ exceptional atoms, counting both atoms of a pair. For $q\geq1$, the concatenated ordinary parameters $W=(w_1,\ldots,w_q)$ lie in a set of volume at most $$\label{eq:cutoff-ellipsoid}
 C_s\{(1+P)(1+q)\}^{C s}
 \left(C\sqrt{1+P/q}\right)^{3q}.$$ The exponential base $C$ is absolute. The containing set depends on the ordinary normals and the graph but not on the numerical contact times or on the exceptional parameters. If $P,q$ are bounded by fixed powers of $|\log\epsilon|$, the prefactor is a fixed power of $|\log\epsilon|$.

Consider the positive low-complexity graph integrals obtained above. Suppose a cut sequence has $q=b-O_K(1)$ ordinary atoms and boundedly many exceptional pieces, whose conditional mass bounds have product $\epsilon^\alpha|\log\epsilon|^{C_*}$. After summing spatial cells and energy bins in a polylogarithmic energy region, its integral is at most $$\label{eq:cutoff-sum}
 \epsilon^\alpha|\log\epsilon|^{C_*}
       (C\tau\sqrt L)^q .$$ The exponential base $C$ depends on the fixed kinetic envelopes and dimension, but not on $K,L$.
